<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kwame Nkrumah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kwame Nkrumah was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>'s independence movement and the first prime minister and president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, which became independent in 1957. He chose the name Ghana in tribute to the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>, though the old empire lay far to the north-west. The choice reflects the empire's lasting symbolic importance in African history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah.jpg" alt="Kwame Nkrumah" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: USSR Post · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kwame_Nkrumah_on_a_Soviet_stamp_(80th_anniversary_of_his_birth)_1989_CPA_6101.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Gold Coast gained independence in 1957, <b>Kwame Nkrumah</b> chose this empire's name precisely to assert a pre-colonial African tradition of statehood and sophistication, seven centuries after the original kingdom had ceased to exist.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kwame Nkrumah</b> won his seat in the Legislative Assembly election of February 1951 while in jail for sedition; the governor, Sir Charles Arden-Clarke, released him and invited him to form a government as 'leader of government business'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Burma and Ceylon followed in 1948, and in the Gold Coast <b>Kwame Nkrumah</b> drew on Gandhi’s example for his campaign of “positive action” in 1950.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>…founders were Josip Broz Tito, the Marxist–Leninist president of Yugoslavia, whose initiative led to the Belgrade conference; Gamal Abdel Nasser of Egypt; Jawaharlal Nehru, prime minister of India; <b>Kwame Nkrumah</b> of Ghana; and Sukarno of Indonesia, host of the Bandung Conference, who gave the movement a significant boost.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kwame Nkrumah</b> argued in Africa Must Unite (1963) that only a continental union government, with a common market, currency and defence, could make independence real and resist what he called neo-colonialism.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>…Acemoglu, Johnson and Robinson trace present differences in wealth and government to colonial institutions, and the word neocolonialism, used by Jean-Paul Sartre in 1956 and by <b>Kwame Nkrumah</b>, Ghana’s first president, in Neo-Colonialism: The Last Stage of Imperialism (1965), names economic and political control that outlasted formal empire.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nkroful, in what is now <a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=4.96,-2.32&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Nkroful on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4486 1978 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kwame Nkrumah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4936 2248h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4936 2248h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=4.96,-2.32&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
